From: FlipperOneEmu
Subject: [PATCH] hw/ufs: a written configuration, applied at the device's initialisation

A UFS device is divided into logical units by its configuration
descriptor, and one written takes effect when the device next
initialises (fDeviceInit), destructively: the device rebuilds its
mapping. The Flipper OS installer provisions a factory-fresh device so
(its "Reprovision UFS": the descriptor written, bBootLunEn set,
fDeviceInit set and waited for, the LUs rescanned), as Rockchip's
USB-plug loader does from the mask ROM. Patch 0017's device took a
written configuration only when it asked for its LUs as they were.

A configuration written is now checked against what the device has
(LUs there, 4 KiB blocks, normal or enhanced1 memory, its allocation
units and enhanced1's ceiling, a shared WriteBooster buffer that keeps
user space), and it reads back as written until fDeviceInit applies
it: its header to the device descriptor, each LU to its unit
descriptor, its drive cut to the capacity its allocation units give
(what it held gone: truncated to nothing, then grown). An LU disabled
no longer answers: REPORT LUNS leaves it out, and a command to it is
answered as one to an LU that is not there.

The device keeps its configuration in its nvram, beside bBootLunEn,
and its LUs take it as they come up. With none kept, they come up as
their properties say (enabled; alloc-units, their drives sized to it),
and the device keeps that. A device can have a capacity of its own
(capacity: its geometry's, which its LUs no longer add up to), and can
come as its maker ships it until it is provisioned (factory: one LU,
all of it, normal memory, its boot feature off, no WriteBooster
buffer, as the Flipper One's Foresee part reads).

